<VI _name="lvai_vi_errors.vi" description="What LabVIEW's Error List shows: every BROKEN VI in memory\2C with the error text and its explanation\2C read in two application instances - the one this VI runs in\2C and the ACTIVE PROJECT's.\0A\0AWHY IT EXISTS. Execution\3AState says a VI is broken and VI Linker Errors names a missing subVI\2C but nothing else in VI Server names WHY - a loose wire\2C an unwired required input\2C a type conflict all read as eBad and nothing more. Two methods the public catalogue does not list do\3A {LV.Application} Get Broken VI List (the NAMES of the broken VIs in that instance) and {LV.VI} Get Errors (per VI\2C Errors - the Error List's own lines\2C such as 'Add\3A Contains unwired or bad terminal' - and Details\2C the text the dialog shows below them). NI's Application Builder uses exactly this pair to explain a failed build.\0A\0ARUN IT OVER ActiveX\2C NOT THROUGH NI's SERVICE. Measured 2026-10-14 as an A/B with one broken VI open in the IDE\3A run over ActiveX the first section listed it with its error\3B run through RunVIAsTopLevel - the AI addon's own application instance - the list was EMPTY with no error. The unwired references below mean 'the instance I run in'\2C and only ActiveX runs this VI in the IDE's main instance.\0A\0AA VI opened inside a project lives in THAT project's instance\2C hence the second section. It answers code 1055 when no project is active\2C which is not a failure of the first section.\0A\0AREAD ONLY. Nothing is opened from disk\3A a broken VI is opened by NAME because it is already in memory\2C and every reference is closed again.">

